Z||000000||GTMike||07-20-2001||01:28 AM||||going to try to post pix of my glue job.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/egrtube3.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/egrtube2.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/egrtube1.JPG"> <P>i already checked to see if it fit. and it does.    so i pulled it off and put another coat of  metal jb weld on it.   i hope it works    what do you guys think.   i broke right at the base of the flang thing.  so i ruffed it up and cleand it real good.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||07-20-2001||06:23 AM||||may be wrong, but I think theres way too much heat for it to last long. I recommend replacing them with the braided aircraft style. may be a little pricey, but last one youll need.||||reg||1
Z||000002||k2m4||07-20-2001||09:05 AM||||You know i used JB weld to repair my coolant tube on the driver side of my car. I didnt want to replace the whole tube so i used that stuff and let me tell you i was super impressed!!!!!!!!!!!. It saved me a ton of money time and aggravation in replacing the whole tube and it was a good excuse to flush and fill the pig. hasnt leaked since, and dried hard as a rock!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||07-20-2001||10:59 AM||||One thing in your favor, is that (it looks like) the repair is on the cooler (plenum) end of the tube.  I have to think that the EGR valve end might tend to see "more" heat (a higher time-temperature product integral, or whatever).  And with the glue repair, you'll know just where to start looking for cracks!<P>Thought about swapping the two gaskets on the ends of the EGR tube to reduce temperatures at the plenum end - putting the one with the orifice at the EGR valve end, but you risk having a <B>worse</B> vacuum leak if the tube cracks again... I wouldn't want to try it, myself. <P>Will it last, and how long?  Time will tell.  And you aren't investing much money in this experimental repair, so it's worth trying.  The biggest investment you're making here is your time, and you're doing us a big favor when you report things like this.  Thanks! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||07-20-2001||11:35 AM||||I brazed my with a swirl port torch, map gas, and flux coated brazing rods from home depot.<P>  since I already had the torch I think the gas and rods cost me about $11 (and I still have most of both left over)<P>  so maybe it only really cost me a buck or two - still holding up after two months.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||07-20-2001||11:36 AM||||hey this isnt just a hollow attempt to get a mention on Paul Harvey, is it?!<P>  (c:||||reg||1
